                "content": "**Notation used throughout**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| \\([A]\\) , \\([I]\\) | instantaneous concentrations of A and the intermediate I |\n| \\([A]_{\\rm T}\\) | total \u201creactant\u2019\u2019 concentration, \\([A]_{\\rm T}=[A]+[I]\\) |\n| \\(K_{\\rm eq}(T)\\) | equilibrium constant for the rapid pre\u2011equilibrium \\(A\\rightleftharpoons I\\) |\n| \\(k_{P}(T),\\,k_{Q}(T)\\) | elementary first\u2011order rate constants for \\(I\\!\\to\\!P\\) and \\(A\\!\\to\\!Q\\) respectively |\n| \\(k_{\\rm obs}(T)\\) | pseudo\u2011first\u2011order disappearance constant of \\([A]_{\\rm T}\\) |\n| \\(r_{P},r_{Q}\\) | instantaneous formation rates of P and Q |\n\nThe Eyring equation (temperature\u2013independent entropy/enthalpy) is  \n\n\\[\nk(T)=\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\;\n      \\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}}{R}\\Bigr)\n      \\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}}{RT}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Expressions for the thermodynamic quantities  \n\n**Equilibrium constant**  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta G^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}&=\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}-T\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}\\\\[2mm]\nK_{\\rm eq}(T)&=\\exp\\!\\Bigl[-\\frac{\\Delta G^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{RT}\\Bigr]\n               =\\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{R}\n                              -\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{RT}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWith the data  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nK_{\\rm eq}(T)=\\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{40.00}{R}\n                         -\\frac{20.00\\times10^{3}}{RT}\\Bigr)}\n\\qquad(R=8.3144626\\;\\text{J\u202fmol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1})\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Elementary rate constants**  \n\nFrom Eq.(1)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nk_{P}(T)&=\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\;\n          \\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{-20.00}{R}\\Bigr)\\;\n          \\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{45.00\\times10^{3}}{RT}\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\nk_{Q}(T)&=\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\;\n          \\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{30.00}{R}\\Bigr)\\;\n          \\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{70.00\\times10^{3}}{RT}\\Bigr)\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The exponentials contain the activation entropies \\(\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P,Q}\\).)\n\n---\n\n**Pseudo\u2011first\u2011order disappearance constant**  \n\nThe total loss of reactant is\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{d[A]_{\\rm T}}{dt}= -k_{Q}[A]-k_{P}[I].\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing the pre\u2011equilibrium \\([I]=K_{\\rm eq}[A]\\),\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{d[A]_{\\rm T}}{dt}= -[A]\\bigl(k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}\\bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\([A]_{\\rm T}=[A]+[I]=[A](1+K_{\\rm eq})\\),\n\n\\[\n[A]=\\frac{[A]_{\\rm T}}{1+K_{\\rm eq}},\n\\qquad\n-\\frac{d[A]_{\\rm T}}{dt}= -[A]_{\\rm T}\\,\n        \\underbrace{\\frac{k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}{1+K_{\\rm eq}}}_{k_{\\rm obs}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k_{\\rm obs}(T)=\\frac{k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}{1+K_{\\rm eq}} } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Product\u2011rate ratio and the temperature of equal rates  \n\nInstantaneous formation rates  \n\n\\[\nr_{P}=k_{P}[I]=k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}[A],\\qquad \nr_{Q}=k_{Q}[A].\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\frac{r_{P}}{r_{Q}}=\\frac{k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}{k_{Q}} } .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(r_{P}=r_{Q}\\) gives the condition  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\rm eq}= \\frac{k_{Q}}{k_{P}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit forms of the three functions.  \nFrom (3) the ratio of the two elementary rate constants simplifies dramatically:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k_{Q}}{k_{P}}\n    =\\exp\\!\\Bigl[\\frac{\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P}}{R}\\Bigr]\\;\n     \\exp\\!\\Bigl[-\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P}}{RT}\\Bigr].\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2) and (9) into (8) and take natural logarithms:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{R}-\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{RT}\n=\n\\frac{\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P}}{R}\n-\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P}}{RT}.\n\\]\n\nCollect the terms containing \\(1/T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}-\n       (\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P})\\Bigr)\n       \\frac{1}{T}\n=\n\\Bigl(\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}-\n       (\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P})\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nFinally\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nT_{\\;r_{P}=r_{Q}}=\n\\frac{\\displaystyle\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}\n      -(\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P})}\n     {\\displaystyle\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}\n      -(\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P})}}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nNo approximations have been made; the result is completely analytical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   Numerical evaluation at **\\(T=500.0\\;{\\rm K}\\)**  \n\nAll constants are inserted in SI units (J\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9, s\u207b\u00b9).  \nThe auxiliary factor that appears in every Eyring expression is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\Bigg|_{500\\;{\\rm K}}\n=\n\\frac{1.380\\,649\\times10^{-23}\\times500}\n     {6.626\\,070\\,15\\times10^{-34}}\n=1.0415\\times10^{13}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}} .\n\\]\n\n| Quantity | Calculation | Value |\n|----------|-------------|-------|\n| \\(K_{\\rm eq}\\) | \\(\\exp\\!\\bigl(40/R-20000/(R\\cdot500)\\bigr)\\) | **0.9973** |\n| \\(k_{P}\\) | \\(1.0415\\times10^{13}\\,\\exp(-20/R)\\,\\exp(-45000/(R\\cdot500))\\) | **\\(2.01\\times10^{7}\\;{\\rm s^{-1}}\\)** |\n| \\(k_{Q}\\) | \\(1.0415\\times10^{13}\\,\\exp(30/R)\\,\\exp(-70000/(R\\cdot500))\\) | **\\(1.89\\times10^{7}\\;{\\rm s^{-1}}\\)** |\n| \\(k_{\\rm obs}\\) | \\((k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq})/(1+K_{\\rm eq})\\) | **\\(1.95\\times10^{7}\\;{\\rm s^{-1}}\\)** |\n| Branching to P | \\(\\displaystyle f_{P}= \\frac{k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}{k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}\\) | **0.515** |\n| Branching to Q | \\(f_{Q}=1-f_{P}\\) | **0.485** |\n| Rate ratio \\(r_{P}/r_{Q}\\) | \\((k_{P}K_{\\rm eq})/k_{Q}\\) | **1.06** |\n\nThe numbers show that at 500\u202fK the two pathways contribute almost equally to the disappearance of the total reactant pool, while slightly more material proceeds through the \\(I\\!\\to\\!P\\) channel.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Apparent activation energy of the overall disappearance  \n\nFrom (6)\n\n\\[\nk_{\\rm obs}= \\frac{k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}{1+K_{\\rm eq}} .\n\\]\n\nTake the logarithm and differentiate with respect to \\(x=1/T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ln k_{\\rm obs}= \\ln(k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq})-\\ln(1+K_{\\rm eq}),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}\\ln k_{\\rm obs}}{{\\rm d}x}\n= \\frac{k_{P}K'}{k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}}\n   -\\frac{K'}{1+K_{\\rm eq}},\n\\qquad K'\\equiv\\frac{{\\rm d}K_{\\rm eq}}{{\\rm d}x}.\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the compact notation  \n\n\\[\nN\\equiv k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq},\\qquad D\\equiv1+K_{\\rm eq}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(N'=k_{P}K'\\) and \\(D'=K'\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}\\ln k_{\\rm obs}}{{\\rm d}x}\n=K'\\,\\frac{B-A}{ND},\n\\qquad (A\\equiv k_{Q},\\;B\\equiv k_{P}).\n\\]\n\nFor the equilibrium constant we have the exact relation  \n\n\\[\n\\ln K_{\\rm eq}= \\frac{\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{R}\n                -\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{RT}\n            =\\alpha-\\beta x,\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nK'=-\\beta K_{\\rm eq},\n\\]\nwith \\(\\beta=\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}/R\\).\n\nPutting everything together,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}\\ln k_{\\rm obs}}{{\\rm d}x}\n= -\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}}{R}\\,\n   K_{\\rm eq}\\,\n   \\frac{k_{P}-k_{Q}}{(k_{Q}+k_{P}K_{\\rm eq})(1+K_{\\rm eq})}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, the apparent activation energy defined in the statement is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nE_{\\rm app}(T)= -R\\frac{{\\rm d}\\ln k_{\\rm obs}}{{\\rm d}(1/T)}\n              = \\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}\\,\n                      K_{\\rm eq}\\,(k_{P}-k_{Q})}\n                     {k_{\\rm obs}\\,(1+K_{\\rm eq})^{2}} } .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nEquation (11) contains **no** assumption that the two pathways combine into a single Arrhenius law; it follows directly from the kinetic scheme.\n\n**Numerical value at 500\u202fK**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}&=20.00\\;{\\rm kJ\\,mol^{-1}}=2.0\\times10^{4}\\ {\\rm J\\,mol^{-1}},\\\\\nK_{\\rm eq}&=0.9973,\\qquad\nk_{P}=2.01\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\\\\\nk_{Q}&=1.89\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\\\\\nk_{\\rm obs}&=1.95\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\\\\\n(1+K_{\\rm eq})^{2}&=(1.9973)^{2}=3.989.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm app}(500\\;{\\rm K})\n= \\frac{2.0\\times10^{4}\\times0.9973\\times(2.01-1.89)\\times10^{7}}\n       {1.95\\times10^{7}\\times3.989}\n\\approx 3.1\\times10^{2}\\ {\\rm J\\,mol^{-1}}\n= \\boxed{0.31\\ {\\rm kJ\\,mol^{-1}} } .\n\\]\n\nThe very small apparent activation energy reflects the fact that, at 500\u202fK, the two competing routes have almost identical rates and the pre\u2011equilibrium constant is close to unity.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Effect of an isotopic substitution  \n\nOnly the activation enthalpies are altered:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P,\\;{\\rm iso}} = 45.00+4.00 = 49.00\\ {\\rm kJ\\,mol^{-1}},\n\\qquad\n\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q,\\;{\\rm iso}} = 70.00+1.00 = 71.00\\ {\\rm kJ\\,mol^{-1}} .\n\\]\n\nAll entropies stay the same, therefore the ratio \\(k_{Q}/k_{P}\\) at any temperature changes to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k_{Q}}{k_{P}}\n= \\exp\\!\\Bigl[\\frac{\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P}}{R}\\Bigr]\\;\n  \\exp\\!\\Bigl[-\\frac{(\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P})\n                 +(\\Delta\\Delta H^{\\ddagger})}{RT}\\Bigr],\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Delta\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}= (+4)-(+1)=+3\\ {\\rm kJ\\,mol^{-1}}\\) is the net extra barrier introduced by the isotope effect.\n\nApplying the general condition (10) with the modified activation enthalpies gives the new temperature at which the two pathways give equal rates:\n\n\\[\nT_{\\;r_{P}=r_{Q}}^{\\;{\\rm iso}}=\n\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}\n      -(\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{Q,\\;{\\rm iso}}\n        -\\Delta H^{\\ddagger}_{P,\\;{\\rm iso}})}\n     {\\Delta S^{\\circ}_{\\rm eq}\n      -(\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{Q}-\\Delta S^{\\ddagger}_{P})}\n=\n\\frac{20.00-(71.00-49.00)}\n     {40.00-(30-(-20))}\n=\n\\frac{20.00-22.00}{40.00-50.00}\n= \\boxed{200\\ {\\rm K}} .\n\\]\n\nThus the temperature at which the product distribution flips is shifted **downward** from 500\u202fK to 200\u202fK because the relative increase in the activation barrier for the P\u2011forming route (four kJ\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9) is larger than that for the Q\u2011forming route (one kJ\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9).  At low temperature the slower, higher\u2011barrier pathway (now the P\u2011path) contributes less, so Q dominates.\n\n**Rate ratio at the original 500\u202fK with the isotopic system**\n\nFirst recompute the two elementary rate constants (Eyring, Eq.\u202f1) with the new enthalpies:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk_{P}^{\\;{\\rm iso}} &=\n\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\,\n\\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{-20}{R}\\Bigr)\\,\n\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{49.00\\times10^{3}}{RT}\\Bigr)\n= 7.95\\times10^{6}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\\\\[2mm]\nk_{Q}^{\\;{\\rm iso}} &=\n\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\,\n\\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{30}{R}\\Bigr)\\,\n\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{71.00\\times10^{3}}{RT}\\Bigr)\n= 1.64\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The factor \\(\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}=1.0415\\times10^{13}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\) is unchanged.)\n\nThe equilibrium constant is unchanged (\\(K_{\\rm eq}=0.9973\\)).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{r_{P}^{\\;{\\rm iso}}}{r_{Q}^{\\;{\\rm iso}}}\n= \\frac{k_{P}^{\\;{\\rm iso}}K_{\\rm eq}}{k_{Q}^{\\;{\\rm iso}}}\n= \\frac{7.95\\times10^{6}\\times0.9973}{1.64\\times10^{7}}\n\\approx \\boxed{0.48}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently, at 500\u202fK the isotopically substituted system now produces **more Q than P**, whereas the non\u2011substituted system gave a slight excess of P.  \n\n**Why the selectivity changes without affecting the pre\u2011equilibrium**\n\nThe pre\u2011equilibrium \\(A\\rightleftharpoons I\\) depends only on the thermodynamics of that step (\u0394H\u00b0, \u0394S\u00b0) which are untouched by the isotope label.  \nProduct selectivity, however, is governed by the competition between the two irreversible elementary steps. Raising the activation enthalpy of the P\u2011forming step more strongly than that of the Q\u2011forming step lowers the rate constant \\(k_{P}\\) relative to \\(k_{Q}\\). Because the observable rate of P formation is proportional to \\(k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}\\) while that of Q is simply \\(k_{Q}\\), the balance \\(k_{P}K_{\\rm eq}=k_{Q}\\) that defines equal product rates shifts to a lower temperature. In short, **isotopic substitution changes the intrinsic kinetic barriers of the two pathways, thereby altering their relative contributions even though the upstream equilibrium remains the same**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Summary of numerical results (500\u202fK)\n\n| Quantity | Non\u2011isotopic | Isotopic (\u0394H\u2021 shifted) |\n|----------|--------------|------------------------|\n| \\(K_{\\rm eq}\\) | 0.9973 | 0.9973 |\n| \\(k_{P}\\) | \\(2.01\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\) | \\(7.95\\times10^{6}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\) |\n| \\(k_{Q}\\) | \\(1.89\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\) | \\(1.64\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\) |\n| \\(k_{\\rm obs}\\) | \\(1.95\\times10^{7}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\) | (can be recomputed similarly) |\n| Branching to P | 51.5\u202f% | 48\u202f% |\n| Branching to Q | 48.5\u202f% | 52\u202f% |\n| \\(r_{P}/r_{Q}\\) | 1.06 | 0.48 |\n| Temperature of equal rates | **500\u202fK** | **200\u202fK** |\n| Apparent activation energy \\(E_{\\rm app}\\) | **0.31\u202fkJ\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9** | (different value if required) |\n\nThese results fulfil all five parts of the problem.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multi-part problem with derivations and calculations.\n\nFirst define equilibrium constant K_eq = exp(-\u0394G\u00b0/RT) where \u0394G\u00b0 = \u0394H\u00b0 - T\u0394S\u00b0. So K_eq = exp[(\u0394S\u00b0/R) - (\u0394H\u00b0/(RT))].\n\nRate constants via Eyring: k_P = (k_B T / h) * exp(\u0394S_P\u2021/R) * exp(-\u0394H_P\u2021/(R T)). Similarly k_Q.\n\nNow system: A \u21cc I fast pre-equilibrium, then I \u2192 P (slow). Also A \u2192 Q directly (slow). The total disappearance of A_T = [A] + [I] follows first order because I quickly equilibrates with A. For pseudo-first-order we have:\n\nd[A_T]/dt = -(k_Q [A]) - (k_P [I]).\n\nBut using equilibrium relation [I] = K_eq [A].\n\nThus d[A_T]/dt = -(k_Q [A]) - (k_P K_eq [A]) = -[A] (k_Q + k_P K_eq).\n\nSince [A] = [A_T]/(1+K_eq)?? Actually [A_T]=[A]+[I]=[A](1+K_eq). So [A] = [A_T]/(1+K_eq). Then plug:\n\nRate = -[A_T]/(1+K_eq) (k_Q + k_P K_eq) = -[A_T] * (k_Q + k_P K_eq)/(1+K_eq).\n\nThus k_obs = (k_Q + k_P K_eq)/(1+K_eq).\n\nAlternatively could express as weighted sum.\n\nNow product rates: r_P = k_P [I] = k_P K_eq [A]; r_Q = k_Q [A]. Ratio r_P/r_Q = (k_P K_eq)/k_Q.\n\nSet equal => k_P K_eq = k_Q => K_eq = k_Q/k_P.\n\nSolve for T where this holds.\n\nNow compute numerically at 500 K.\n\nCompute constants:\n\nGiven \u0394H_eq = 20.00 kJ/mol = 20000 J/mol. \u0394S_eq = 40.00 J/(mol\u00b7K). So K_eq(T) = exp(\u0394S/R - \u0394H/(R T)). Compute exponent: \u0394S/R = 40/8.3144626 = 4.809... Let's compute precisely: 40 / 8.3144626 = 4.8089. \u0394H/(R T) = 20000 / (8.3144626*500) = 20000 / 4157.2313 = 4.812... Wait compute denominator: R*T = 8.3144626*500 = 4157.2313 J/mol. So \u0394H/(R T) = 20000/4157.2313 = 4.8116. So exponent = 4.8089 - 4.8116 = -0.0027 approx. So K_eq \u2248 exp(-0.0027) = 0.9973 ~0.997.\n\nNow compute k_P and k_Q.\n\nk_P = (k_B T/h) * exp(\u0394S_P\u2021/R) * exp(-\u0394H_P\u2021/(R T))\n\nFirst factor: k_B T/h = (1.380649e-23 * 500) / (6.62607015e-34) = (6.903245e-21) / (6.62607e-34) = 1.0418e13? Let's compute: 6.903245e-21 / 6.62607e-34 = 1.0415e13 s^-1. Good.\n\nNow exp(\u0394S_P\u2021/R): \u0394S_P\u2021 = -20 J/mol\u00b7K. Divide by R: -20/8.3144626 = -2.404. Exp(-2.404) = 0.0905.\n\nexp(-\u0394H_P\u2021/(R T)): \u0394H_P\u2021 = 45000 J/mol. \u0394H/(R T) = 45000 / 4157.2313 = 10.822. Negative sign gives exp(-10.822) = 2.13e-5.\n\nMultiply: 1.0415e13 * 0.0905 * 2.13e-5 = 1.0415e13 * (0.0905*2.13e-5) = 1.0415e13 * (1.931e-6) \u2248 2.009e7 s^-1. Let's compute more accurately: 0.0905*2.13e-5 = 1.93065e-6. Times 1.0415e13 = 2.014e7 s^-1. So k_P \u2248 2.0\u00d710^7 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nNow k_Q: \u0394S_Q\u2021 = 30 J/mol\u00b7K. \u0394S/R = 30/8.3144626 = 3.608. exp(3.608) = 36.9.\n\n\u0394H_Q\u2021 = 70000 J/mol. \u0394H/(R T) = 70000/4157.2313 = 16.828. exp(-16.828) = 4.94e-8.\n\nMultiply: 1.0415e13 * 36.9 * 4.94e-8 = 1.0415e13 * (36.9*4.94e-8) = 1.0415e13 * (1.816e-6) = 1.891e7 s\u207b\u00b9. Wait compute: 36.9*4.94e-8 = 1.817e-6. Times 1.0415e13 = 1.892e7 s\u207b\u00b9. So k_Q \u2248 1.9\u00d710^7 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nInteresting both similar magnitude.\n\nNow compute k_obs = (k_Q + k_P K_eq)/(1+K_eq). Since K_eq\u22480.9973, close to 1.\n\nCompute numerator: k_Q + k_P*K_eq = 1.892e7 + 2.014e7*0.9973 = 1.892e7 + 2.008e7 = 3.898e7 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nDenominator: 1+K_eq = 1.9973.\n\nSo k_obs = 3.898e7 / 1.9973 = 1.952e7 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nNow branching fractions: fraction to P = (rate to P)/(total consumption rate) = r_P / (k_Q[A] + k_P[I])? But easier: fraction of disappearance that goes to P equals (k_P K_eq)/(k_Q + k_P K_eq). Because proportion of total rate contributed by P path. So f_P = (k_P K_eq)/(k_Q + k_P K_eq). Using numbers: numerator = 2.014e7*0.9973 = 2.008e7. Denominator = 3.898e7. So f_P = 0.515. Fraction to Q = 1 - f_P = 0.485.\n\nCheck ratio r_P/r_Q = (k_P K_eq)/k_Q = 2.008e7 / 1.892e7 = 1.061. So about 1.06.\n\nNow find temperature where r_P = r_Q i.e., K_eq = k_Q/k_P.\n\nDefine function f(T) = K_eq(T) - k_Q(T)/k_P(T) = 0.\n\nNeed analytic expression.\n\nK_eq(T) = exp(\u0394S_eq/R - \u0394H_eq/(R T))\n\nk_Q/k_P = [ (k_B T/h) exp(\u0394S_Q\u2021/R) exp(-\u0394H_Q\u2021/(R T)) ] / [ (k_B T/h) exp(\u0394S_P\u2021/R) exp(-\u0394H_P\u2021/(R T)) ] = exp[(\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021)/R] * exp[-(\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021)/(R T)]\n\nBecause the common prefactor cancels. So ratio simplifies nicely! Indeed k_Q/k_P = exp[ (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021)/R ] * exp[ -(\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021)/(R T) ].\n\nThus condition K_eq = k_Q/k_P leads to:\n\nexp(\u0394S_eq/R - \u0394H_eq/(R T)) = exp[ (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021)/R ] * exp[ -(\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021)/(R T) ].\n\nTake logs:\n\n\u0394S_eq/R - \u0394H_eq/(R T) = (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021)/R - (\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021)/(R T)\n\nMultiply by R:\n\n\u0394S_eq - \u0394H_eq/T = (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021) - (\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021)/T\n\nRearrange terms: bring T terms together.\n\n\u0394S_eq - (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021) = (\u0394H_eq - (\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021))/T\n\nThus\n\nT = (\u0394H_eq - (\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021)) / (\u0394S_eq - (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021))\n\nThat's a simple linear solution! Nice.\n\nPlug numbers:\n\n\u0394H_eq = 20.00 kJ = 20000 J\n\n\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021 = 70 - 45 = 25 kJ = 25000 J\n\n\u0394H_eq - (\u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021) = 20000 - 25000 = -5000 J\n\n\u0394S_eq = 40 J/K\n\n\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021 = 30 - (-20) = 50 J/K\n\n\u0394S_eq - (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021) = 40 - 50 = -10 J/K\n\nThus T = (-5000)/(-10) = 500 K. Interesting! So equality occurs exactly at 500 K given these parameters. Good.\n\nNow for isotopic substitution: \u0394H_P\u2021_iso = 45+4 = 49 kJ; \u0394H_Q\u2021_iso = 70+1 = 71 kJ. Activation entropies unchanged. So new ratio k_Q/k_P changes accordingly.\n\nCompute new \u0394H_Q\u2021 - \u0394H_P\u2021 = 71 - 49 = 22 kJ = 22000 J.\n\nNow use same formula but with new \u0394H values.\n\n\u0394H_eq - (\u0394H_Q\u2021_iso - \u0394H_P\u2021_iso) = 20000 - 22000 = -2000 J.\n\n\u0394S term unchanged: \u0394S_eq - (\u0394S_Q\u2021 - \u0394S_P\u2021) = -10 J/K still.\n\nThus T_iso = (-2000)/(-10) = 200 K.\n\nSo equality moves to lower temperature (200 K). That seems plausible.\n\nNow compute r_P/r_Q at 500 K with iso.\n\nWe can compute k_P_iso and k_Q_iso using Eyring with new \u0394H.\n\nLet's compute k_P_iso: \u0394H_P\u2021_iso = 49 kJ = 49000 J. \u0394H/(R T) = 49000/4157.23 = 11.79. exp(-11.79) = 8.44e-6.\n\nk_P_iso = (k_B T/h) * exp(\u0394S_P\u2021/R) * exp(-\u0394H_P\u2021_iso/(R T)) = 1.0415e13 * 0.0905 * 8.44e-6 = 1.0415e13 * (7.64e-7) = 7.95e6 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nk_Q_iso: \u0394H_Q\u2021_iso = 71 kJ = 71000 J. \u0394H/(R T) = 71000/4157.23 = 17.07. exp(-17.07) = 4.26e-8.\n\nk_Q_iso = 1.0415e13 * 36.9 * 4.26e-8 = 1.0415e13 * (1.572e-6) = 1.637e7 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nNow K_eq unchanged (since pre-equilibrium unaffected) = 0.9973.\n\nRatio r_P/r_Q = (k_P_iso * K_eq)/k_Q_iso = (7.95e6 * 0.9973)/1.637e7 = 7.93e6 / 1.637e7 = 0.484.\n\nThus at 500 K, r_P < r_Q now (more Q). Good.\n\nNow part (4): derive E_app(T) = -R d ln k_obs / d(1/T). We have k_obs = (k_Q + k_P K_eq)/(1+K_eq). Take derivative.\n\nLet\u2019s denote A = k_Q, B = k_P, C = K_eq. Then k_obs = (A + B C)/(1+C). Write as numerator N = A + B C, denominator D = 1 + C.\n\nThen ln k_obs = ln N - ln D.\n\nDerivative w.r.t x = 1/T: d ln k_obs / dx = (N'/N) - (D'/D).\n\nWhere prime denotes derivative wrt x.\n\nWe know N' = B * C' (since A independent of T). D' = C'.\n\nThus:\n\nd ln k_obs / dx = (B C' / N) - (C' / D) = C' (B/N - 1/D).\n\nSimplify: = C' * ( (B D - N) / (N D) ).\n\nBut N = A + B C, D = 1 + C.\n\nCompute B D - N = B(1+C) - (A + B C) = B + B C - A - B C = B - A.\n\nThus B D - N = B - A.\n\nTherefore d ln k_obs / dx = C' * (B - A) / (N D).\n\nRecall N D = (A + B C)(1 + C) = (A + B C)(1 + C). Could also write as (A + B C)(1 + C).\n\nNow C' = dK_eq/dx = dK_eq/dT * dT/dx. Since x = 1/T, dx = -dT/T^2, so dT/dx = -T^2. Alternatively differentiate directly: K_eq = exp(\u03b1 - \u03b2/x) where \u03b1 = \u0394S_eq/R, \u03b2 = \u0394H_eq/R. Indeed K_eq = exp(\u03b1 - \u03b2 x). Then ln K_eq = \u03b1 - \u03b2 x. Derivative wrt x: d ln K_eq / dx = -\u03b2. Thus C' = K_eq * (-\u03b2) = -\u03b2 K_eq.\n\nWhere \u03b2 = \u0394H_eq / R.\n\nThus C' = - (\u0394H_eq / R) * K_eq.\n\nNow B - A = k_P - k_Q.\n\nThus d ln k_obs / dx = - (\u0394H_eq / R) K_eq (k_P - k_Q) / (N D).\n\nNow E_app = -R * (d ln k_obs / dx) = (\u0394H_eq) K_eq (k_P - k_Q) / (N D).\n\nBut note N D = (A + B C)(1 + C) = (k_Q + k_P K_eq)(1 + K_eq) = k_obs (1 + K_eq)^2? Wait k_obs = (A + B C)/(1 + C). Multiply numerator and denominator: (A + B C) = k_obs (1 + C). So N = k_obs (1 + C). Therefore N D = k_obs (1 + C)*(1 + C) = k_obs (1 + K_eq)^2.\n\nThus E_app = (\u0394H_eq) K_eq (k_P - k_Q) / [k_obs (1 + K_eq)^2].\n\nAlternatively we can express in terms of k_obs etc.\n\nNow evaluate at 500 K using earlier numbers.\n\nCompute k_P - k_Q = 2.014e7 - 1.892e7 = 0.122e7 = 1.22e6 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nK_eq = 0.9973.\n\n\u0394H_eq = 20000 J/mol.\n\nk_obs = 1.952e7 s\u207b\u00b9.\n\n(1 + K_eq) = 1.9973. Square = 3.989.\n\nNow numerator: \u0394H_eq * K_eq * (k_P - k_Q) = 20000 * 0.9973 * 1.22e6 = 20000 * 1.216e6 \u2248 2.432e10 J\u00b7s\u207b\u00b9? Let's compute: 0.9973*1.22e6 = 1.216e6. Times 20000 = 2.432e10 J\u00b7s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nDenominator: k_obs * (1+K_eq)^2 = 1.952e7 * 3.989 = 7.777e7.\n\nThus E_app = 2.432e10 / 7.777e7 = 312.6 J/mol = 0.313 kJ/mol. That's tiny! Something off maybe due to small difference between k_P and k_Q leading to small apparent activation energy. Let's double-check calculations.\n\nMaybe we should keep units consistent: \u0394H_eq in J/mol, k in s\u207b\u00b9, k_obs in s\u207b\u00b9, result in J/mol per (1/K)?? Actually E_app has units of energy (J/mol). So fine.\n\nOur computed 0.313 kJ/mol is very low, meaning overall disappearance almost temperature independent near 500 K because the two pathways have similar rates and pre-equilibrium constant near 1.\n\nLet's verify numeric steps more precisely.\n\nCompute k_P = 2.014e7, k_Q = 1.892e7, diff = 1.22e6.\n\nK_eq = 0.9973.\n\nProduct numerator: \u0394H_eq * K_eq * diff = 20000 * 0.9973 * 1.22e6 = 20000 * (0.9973*1.22e6) = 20000 * (1.216... e6) = 2.432e10 J\u00b7s\u207b\u00b9.\n\nDenominator: k_obs * (1+K_eq)^2 = 1.952e7 * (1.9973)^2. Compute (1.9973)^2 = 3.989? Let's compute precisely: 1.9973^2 = (2 - 0.0027)^2 = 4 - 2*2*0.0027 + 0.0027^2 \u2248 4 - 0.0108 + 7.29e-6 \u2248 3.9892. Good.\n\nMultiply: 1.952e7 * 3.989 = 7.775e7.\n\nDivide: 2.432e10 / 7.775e7 = 312.5 J/mol = 0.313 kJ/mol.\n\nSeems correct.\n\nIf we want to present E_app in kJ/mol: 0.313 kJ/mol.\n\nNow part (5) explanation: isotope substitution raises \u0394H\u2021 for both pathways, but relatively larger increase for P (4 kJ vs 1 kJ). This shifts balance such that at higher temperatures P becomes less favorable relative to Q, moving equality to lower temperature. Provide reasoning.\n\nNow produce final answer with clear derivations.\n\nWill include formulas, steps, numerical results.\n\nAlso mention that the derived expression for E_app avoids assuming single Arrhenius behavior.\n\nProceed to final."
            }
